        <OtherAbstract Type="PIP" Language="eng">
            <AbstractText>Over a 10-year period 2407 women were prescribed contraceptives: 2046 used the IUD DANA (Super, Special, Cor, Fix, and Lux), and 280 used hormonal contraceptives (Biogest, Brombiogest, Chlorbiogest, and Norbiogest). 51 women used Depo-Provera and 30 used the domestic Antiko-capiky, both chemical preparations. Pregnancy occurred with the IUDs in 9.03% of the cases, with the hormonal preparations in 2.9% of the cases, with Depo-Provera in .0% of the cases, and with Antiko-capiky in .3% of the cases. The IUDs caused complications in 8% hormonal contraceptives in 5%, Depo-Provera in 15%, and Antiko-capiky in 13%. The findings suggested that the higher the effectiveness of a method in preventing pregnancy, the greater the risk of detrimental side-effects, either directly from the area of the reproductive organs, or from the secondary connective systems. Study of the biochemical, endocrinological, and morphological factors and the physiological functions behind each of the contraceptive-related complications is deemed necessary in order to understand them in regard to each of the mentioned methods.</AbstractText>
        </OtherAbstract>
